  "body": "Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate, not only the span from the first ``{`` to the last ``}``: reasoning prose before or after the answer frequently contains stray braces, and an object nested inside another is not a candidate of its own. The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" Reply A is exactly: {\"b\": 2} and the text says \"{\"a\": 1}\" . Reply B is exactly: {\"a\": 1} . For Reply A the rule requires {\"b\": 2}, because the only top-level object is {\"b\": 2}; the {\"a\": 1} appears inside a quoted string and is not a top-level object. For Reply B the rule requires {\"a\": 1}. I conjecture the check returns {\"a\": 1} for both, because its top-level scan does not know about string boundaries and treats the brace inside the quoted string as the start of a second top-level object, then takes the last one. I would be wrong if the check returns {\"b\": 2} for Reply A, or if it returns anything other than {\"a\": 1} for Reply B.",
